{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>The outcry of Sodom and Gomorrah, for it is great.</b> Because they sinned in licentiousness — for a person's desire is great and ever-increasing, since a small member in a person makes him hungry [yet is never satisfied]. And likewise they sinned in robbery and violence, for therefore they enacted among themselves statutes that are not good, ordinances by which one cannot live, as is well known from the false judges of Sodom. And the lust for money is also great and ever-increasing, for ״אֹהֵב כֶּסֶף לֹא יִשְׂבַּע כֶּסֶף״ (Ecclesiastes 5:9). Therefore the expression ״כִּי רָבָּה״ — 'for it is great' — was used, in the same manner as was said of the generation of the Flood: ״וַיַּרְא ה׳ כִּי רַבָּה רָעַת הָאָדָם״ (Genesis 6:5), as was explained in its place (Genesis 6:5). And so too here its meaning is that it was great and ever-increasing — and not according to Rashi's interpretation. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 49:6) concerning the matter of a certain girl, etc. This midrash too is encompassed in both senses, for that girl cried out because of what they did to her for giving flour to a poor person — and that is the proliferation of the lust for money, which brought them to this trait. Behold, the outcry of a betrothed girl who cried out in the city with none to save her — and this is what is said: ״זַעֲקַת סְדֹם״, 'the outcry of Sodom' — behold, the outcry of the violated girls; behold, the outcry of the many for justice, and behold, outcry (Isaiah 5:7)." "And a proof for our words from what is said: ״הַאַף תִּסְפֶּה צַדִּיק עִם רָשָׁע?״ — 'Will You indeed sweep away the righteous with the wicked?' From where did Abraham learn this? Rather, because he understood from the statement ״זַעֲקַת סְדֹם כִּי רָבָּה״ that the matter concerned licentiousness, and wherever you find licentiousness, a plague (Andarlamosya) comes upon the world and kills both the good and the wicked (Jerusalemite Talmud, Sotah 1:5) — because the righteous ought to have departed from that defiled place, for there is no sin that defiles even the land like licentiousness, as it is written in the passage of forbidden relations (Leviticus 18:27): ״וַתִּטְמָא הָאָרֶץ״. For even idolatry does not defile the land, for land held in perpetuity is not rendered forbidden, for 'their gods are not upon the mountains' (Babylonian Talmud, Avodah Zarah 45a). And because the righteous did not depart from within that defiled place, it is just that they be punished together with them — for in every place where there is a boundary of forbidden sexual relations, there you find holiness. And here it is said ״וְנֵדְעָה אֹתָם״, behold, they were defiled also through male intercourses. And Abraham argued about this: Granted, if You punish by means of an agent, that is, Andarlamosya, which does not distinguish between the good and the wicked, since there is also in the good an aspect of sin — that they dwelt among them. But You said ״אֵרְדָה נָּא וְאֶרְאֶה״ and so forth — if so, licentiousness is certainly not the main point, for there is no place at all there for the Shekhinah, and if so, far be it from You — it would be profane to You not to distinguish between the righteous and the wicked, for even if there is an aspect of sin also in the righteous, none the less justice does not dictate that they be equal to the wicked in punishment, ״וְהָיָה כַצַּדִּיק כָּרָשָׁע״, 'that the righteous should be as the wicked.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The final citation of Isaiah 5:7 is given by the Kli Yakar as 'והנה משפח' (literally 'and behold, a vine-branch'), which is the word appearing in that verse in the parable of the vineyard; I have rendered it literally as 'outcry' to match the sense of זעקה he is expounding, though the word itself (משפח) in context means 'wild grapes' or according to some 'stench.' The phrase 'אבר קטן באדם משביעו רעב' is a well-known rabbinic idiom: the small organ (the penis) is never satisfied.", 1 "The Aramaic/Greek loanword 'אנדרלמוסיא' (Andarlamosya) refers to an indiscriminate plague or pestilence that kills without distinguishing between righteous and wicked; I have retained it transliterated. The verse citation 'והיה כצדיק כרשע' is from Genesis 18:25, Abraham's own challenge to HaShem, which the Kli Yakar here presents as the conclusion of Abraham's argument."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:57.157160787Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}